feature you can see what the product does and where it stands
  :Scry opens one buffer holding every feature, checked against the code the
  moment you look. Features carry the state their evidence adds up to; the
  header leads with them and puts claim counts underneath as the evidence.

  command Scry
    The surface itself. Opening it is the whole gesture — everything else
    here is what it renders.
  command ScryCheck
  def lua/scry/glass.lua:open
  def lua/scry/glass.lua:render
  def lua/scry/feature.lua:verdict
  def lua/scry/feature.lua:tally
  def lua/scry/header.lua:header
  exercises
    tests/feature_spec.lua:features lead line one
    tests/glass_spec.lua

feature you can describe a change before you make it
  Write the claim for what you want and it comes back absent, which is not a
  fault — it is the work, named, in the same vocabulary as everything already
  true. If you cannot state it, you do not know what you are asking for yet.

  def lua/scry/map.lua:parse
  def lua/scry/map.lua:footprint
  exercises
    tests/map_spec.lua
    tests/feature_spec.lua:a feature may be named with no evidence

feature the description can be edited without losing anything
  The glass is the map file as a buffer, and a parse/serialize round trip is
  byte-identical, so editing beliefs can never quietly drop one.

  def lua/scry/map.lua:parse
  def lua/scry/map.lua:serialize
  exercises
    tests/map_spec.lua

feature you can conjure a change you described
  :Conjure on an absent claim seeds the quickfix list and hands off to
  conjurer. The check is conjured before the code, and the code request never
  sees the check or this feature's prohibitions.

  def lua/scry/cascade.lua:build
  def lua/scry/cascade.lua:seed
  exercises
    tests/cascade_spec.lua

feature a rule is told up front and enforced after
  A feature's never-claims ride along with every cast, so the generator can
  follow them — and the same patterns are re-checked against the code once
  it lands, so a rule broken anyway is caught with the line that proves it.

  def lua/scry/resolvers/ts_rg.lua:check_never
  def lua/scry/cascade.lua:seed
  exercises
    tests/cascade_spec.lua:the feature's prohibition rides in the intent

feature you can prove a change works, and know when the proof goes stale
  :ScryExercise runs the specs behind exercises claims and records what each
  run depended on. Checking never executes anything, and a pass whose
  dependencies have moved degrades rather than lying.

  def lua/scry/run.lua:specs
  def lua/scry/runs.lua:stale
  def lua/scry/runs.lua:fingerprint
  exercises
    tests/exercises_spec.lua:checking did NOT run the test command

feature you can find what the description does not cover
  Every feature can read done while the map describes a fraction of the
  product. :ScryUnclaimed lists the files no feature claims, which is what
  makes "everything done" falsifiable.

  def lua/scry/divergence.lua:unclaimed
  def lua/scry/divergence.lua:to_quickfix
  exercises
    tests/divergence_spec.lua

feature the glass can check any language it has an engine for
  Claims are decided by a resolver behind an interface: treesitter for lua
  definitions, ripgrep for references and prohibitions. Fidelity travels with
  every verdict, so the glass can never render a stronger word than the engine
  that produced it — and a language with no engine reads unchecked, not passed.

  def lua/scry/resolver.lua:check
  def lua/scry/resolver.lua:register
  def lua/scry/check.lua:run
  def lua/scry/resolvers/ts_rg.lua:check_contains
  def lua/scry/resolvers/ts_rg.lua:check_never
  exercises
    tests/resolver_spec.lua
    tests/check_spec.lua

feature a project carries its own settings
  Which files count as product, and how to run one spec, are facts about a
  repo rather than about you. A repo states them in .scry/config.json and
  wins — except for the one key it must never set, which is refused aloud.

  def lua/scry/project.lua:load
  def lua/scry/project.lua:resolve
  exercises
    tests/project_spec.lua

feature you can find out whether scry can actually see your project
  :checkhealth scry reports the two legs the resolver stands on, where the
  map lives, and whether conjurer is installed — before a missing dependency
  shows up as a wall of unchecked verdicts.

  def lua/scry/health.lua:check
  def lua/scry/init.lua:setup
  module plugin/scry.lua

feature you can have the features drafted for you
  :ScryDraft sends the files no feature claims to a conjurer and the features
  come back into the glass as ordinary text — checked like anything you
  typed, and nothing reaches the map file until you write it. The machine
  types; you decide.

  def lua/scry/recover.lua:build
  def lua/scry/recover.lua:draft
  exercises
    tests/recover_spec.lua
